    <string name="learn_app_toolbar_notes_menu">You can press and hold the Notes button to open the Notes menu, where you can clear or compute notes and toggle their visibility</string>
    <string name="learn_sudoku_rules">Sudoku rules</string>
    <string name="sudoku_rules_mistakes">There is a mistake here, can you spot it? Maybe even two</string>
    <string name="sudoku_rules_mistakes_highlight">Highlight mistakes</string>
    <string name="sudoku_rules_mistakes_explanation">The first mistake is two 2s in the sixth box. Remember the rule that there can\'t be double values in a box? The second mistake is the number 6 in row 2, column 8 (R2C8), because there is already a 6 in that column (R5C8)</string>
    <string name="naked_pairs_title">Naked pairs</string>
    <string name="naked_pairs_explanation">Two cells share the same two pencil marks. We can\'t tell which is 2 and which is 3 yet.\nHowever, Sudoku rules state each box must have exactly one \'2\' and one \'3\', so one of these cells must be 2 and the other 3.</string>
    <string name="naked_pairs_end">This is useful because there are other \'2\' or \'3\' pencil marks in this box. We can remove them, as no other cell in the box can be 2 or 3.</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_title">Basic</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_1">Let\'s look at box number 7. Four cells are already filled.</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_2">Now, consider the number 8. The 8 in row 4, column 3 (R4C3) blocks the entire column within this box, so an 8 cannot be placed there.</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_3">Also, the 8 in R7C5 blocks the entire row within this box.</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_4">We are left with only one cell where the number 8 can go.</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_5">The same logic applies to rows and columns. Look at the first row. The 4 in R7C3 blocks the 3rd column. The 4 in R3C5 blocks two columns (4 and 6) because there can be no other 4s in that box. R6C7 blocks column 7.</string>
    <string name="learn_basic_6">As a result, only one cell in the first row can contain the number 4: R1C3.</string>
    <string name="learn_hidden_pairs_title">Hidden pairs</string>
    <string name="learn_hidden_pairs_1">Hidden pairs are like naked pairs, but hidden among other candidates. These two cells share 6 and 9 as possible values, and they are the only cells in this box that can hold 6 or 9.</string>
    <string name="learn_hidden_pairs_2">Since 6 and 9 must be in these two cells, we can eliminate all other candidates from them. This logic also applies to rows and columns.</string>
